## REASONING

The request is deceptively simple ("resolve elevation bands with a justified N") but hits the framework's central execution limit head-on. Elevation in a mountain basin like the Upper Yakima (500–2400 m, ~1900 m relief) matters almost entirely through *climate*: temperature lapse rates, the rain–snow transition, orographic precipitation, and snowpack accumulation/melt timing. All of that reaches the land surface through the **atmospheric forcing**. The framework runs single-column ELM with the forcing it is given; it has no elevation-downscaling (lapse-rate) capability. So if I place columns across elevation bands but drive them all with the same forcing, the columns will differ only by their soil column — I will have measured a *soil* effect and mislabeled it "elevation." That is the trap step 2 warns about, and it must be stated plainly.

So the honest verdict is **partial**. The stack CAN resolve the soil control on water partitioning across the sampled landscape, and it CAN run a column at any elevation. It CANNOT, with uniform forcing, attribute partitioning differences to elevation/snow — the mechanism the user actually cares about in a snow-dominated basin. That requires elevation-downscaled forcing (and, to validate against USGS gauges, runoff routing/aggregation).

Two goals: (a) mechanistic — how partitioning varies across the basin's elevation/soil structure; (b) validation — SNOTEL (SWE, in-domain, co-located per site) and USGS gauges (streamflow, in-domain but requires routing to be comparable).

Sampling: stratify by elevation band × dominant soil, oversampling the rain–snow transition (~1200–1800 m) where the snow mechanism — and its sensitivity to any future forcing downscaling — is strongest. I keep N small and defensible: ~5 elevation bands × ~2 dominant soils, with light replication in the transition, plus validation columns pinned to SNOTEL sites and gauge-representative locations.

